## Why This Is Asked
Tests for per-user feature flags are common in SaaS apps. This question checks the ability to seed data, perform API-driven state changes, and verify UI behavior across login and page reloads in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Per-user feature flags and API seed/reset
- End-to-end UI verification of dynamic layouts
- Test isolation and deterministic CI runs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example using Playwright-like syntax (pseudocode)
// seedUser(), setFlag(), login() are helper utilities
test('compact dashboard toggling', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const { userId, email, password } = await seedUser(request);
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', true);
  await login(page, email, password);
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', false);
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-full')).toBeVisible();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you guard against flaky UI assertions caused by caching?
- How would you extend the test to cover multiple user segments (roles/regions) and parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for multi-tenant isolation are foundational for SaaS apps. The goal is to confirm UI and API boundaries prevent cross-tenant data leakage, a common subtle bug.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy data isolation
- Role-based access control (RBAC)
- Deterministic fixtures for repeatable runs
- UI vs API boundary testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for tenant isolation
describe('Tenant isolation', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    seedTenants(['A','B']);
  });
  it('creates user in A and enforces isolation', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.login('adminA','A');
    cy.visit('/admin/users');
    cy.get('[data-cy=create-user]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=username]').type('newuser');
    cy.get('[data-cy=save]').click();
    cy.contains('newuser').should('exist');
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=A'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.true;
    });
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=B'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.false;
    });
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this for 100 tenants while keeping tests fast?
- How would you handle flaky network simulations and ensure determinism across runs?

## Why This Is Asked
Testing offline-first sync across devices is a realistic challenge for consumer apps with data integrity concerns, multi-device usage, and eventual consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first architecture: Data is persisted locally and synced when connectivity is restored
- Conflict resolution strategy: Merge (combine changes) vs. Last-Write-Wins (most recent timestamp wins)
- Test isolation across devices: Each Playwright context maintains separate local storage and cookies
- Deterministic network shaping: Use Playwright's `route` or Cypress's `server` to intercept and delay requests

## Code Example
```javascript
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('offline-first sync with conflict resolution', async ({ browser }) => {
  // Create two isolated device contexts
  const ctxA = await browser.newContext();
  const ctxB = await browser.newContext();

  const pageA = await ctxA.newPage();
  const pageB = await ctxB.newPage();

  // Simulate offline on both devices
  await pageA.route('**/api/**', route => route.abort('failed'));
  await pageB.route('**/api/**', route => route.abort('failed'));

  // Device A creates a note offline
  await pageA.goto('/app');
  await pageA.fill('[data-testid="note-input"]', 'Note from A');
  await pageA.click('[data-testid="save"]');

  // Device B creates a note offline
  await pageB.goto('/app');
  await pageB.fill('[data-testid="note-input"]', 'Note from B');
  await pageB.click('[data-testid="save"]');

  // Restore connectivity
  await pageA.unrouteAll();
  await pageB.unrouteAll();

  // Wait for sync and verify
  await pageA.reload();
  await pageB.reload();

  await expect(pageA.locator('[data-testid="notes"]')).toContainText('Note from A');
  await expect(pageA.locator('[data-testid="notes"]')).toContainText('Note from B');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 5+ devices?
- How would you verify conflict resolution for concurrent edits to the same note?
- How would you test partial sync failures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for offline-first flows are common in modern web apps and require practical use of network emulation, event queues, and deterministic conflict handling. This question probes hands-on ability to design reliable E2E tests that stay robust under connectivity changes.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Network emulation in Playwright or Cypress
- Backend API mocking and idempotent sync
- Deterministic conflict resolution policy
- Data seeding for repeatable tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test skeleton
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('offline create and sync with deterministic merge', async ({ browser }) => {
  const context = await browser.newContext();
  const page = await context.newPage();
  // seed data via API (omitted for brevity)
  // go offline
  await context.setOffline(true);
  await page.goto('https://app.example');
  await page.fill('#title', 'Offline note');
  await page.fill('#body', 'content');
  await page.click('#save');
  await expect(page.locator('#status')).toHaveText('Queued');
  // go online and sync
  await context.setOffline(false);
  const resp = await page.waitForResponse('/api/notes');
  // assume server returns created note with serverId
  await expect(resp.status()).toBe(201);
  // verify note appears and is synced
  await expect(page.locator('.note-title').last()).toHaveText('Offline note');
  // optional conflict scenario in a second tab would be tested similarly
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this test for multiple concurrent offline edits across tabs?
- How would you ensure test reliability when the backend response latency varies?

## Why This Is Asked
